# Suppression rules for scanning this repository with its own scanner.
#
# Format:  <path glob>[:<rule id>]   — either half may be omitted.
# Findings matching a rule are dropped from the report entirely.
#
# Everything below is a credential-shaped string that exists *because* this is a
# secret scanner: fixtures the detector must fire on, and documentation showing
# what a finding looks like. They are synthetic and were never issued.

# Detector test fixtures: these are the inputs that prove the rules work. Scoped
# to the whole suite rather than named files, because any new detector test will
# need a credential-shaped fixture too.
tests/**
src/evaluation/security_benchmark/**
# The benchmark's path before the LoRA pipeline was removed. `--history` finds
# its fixtures under the name they had at the time, not the one they have now.
src/ml/evaluation/security_benchmark/**

# The live-fire harness builds the credentials it plants, so its source contains
# a literal PEM private-key header and the provider prefixes the rules
# fingerprint. (This comment says "PEM private-key header" rather than spelling
# it out, because spelling it out made this file a finding — which is the rule
# working correctly.) Every value it emits is generated at run time from
# `secrets.token_*` — there is no key here, only the shape of one. Scoped to the
# one file rather than to `src/evaluation/**`, so a real credential committed
# anywhere else under evaluation is still reported.
src/evaluation/run_live_eval.py

# Documentation examples (README curl samples, the guide's walkthrough).
docs/**:github-pat
README.md:github-pat

# The rule table itself contains example token shapes in its explanations. The
# older path is where it lived before the package was renamed — `--history`
# finds it under the name it had at the time, not the one it has now.
src/reposec/detectors/rules.py
src/security/rules.py

# B104 fires on the literal "0.0.0.0" wherever it appears. In secrets.py it is
# an entry in the set of hosts a connection string cannot meaningfully point at
# — the detector reasoning about binding, not code binding to anything. Scoped
# to the one rule so a real bind in this file would still be reported.
src/reposec/detectors/secrets.py:B104

# docker-compose ships throwaway local-dev Postgres credentials on purpose;
# they are not reachable outside the compose network. The two older paths are
# where the same file lived before the layout was flattened — `--history` finds
# it under the name it had at the time, not the one it has now.
deploy/docker-compose.yml:database-connection-string
infra/docker-compose.yml:database-connection-string
docker-compose.yml:database-connection-string
